WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.415
Lesson 0.6.

00:00:02.415 --> 00:00:04.870
Networks, gas and your first transfer.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.921
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:06.921 --> 00:00:09.193
able to move crypto from the exchange to

00:00:09.193 --> 00:00:11.743
your own wallet safely, on the right network,

00:00:11.743 --> 00:00:13.295
starting with a test amount.

00:00:14.595 --> 00:00:15.885
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.885 --> 00:00:17.570
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.285
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:20.285 --> 00:00:21.810
Then, your checklist.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.972
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:25.272 --> 00:00:29.419
Step by step: First, Networks (chains): the same token

00:00:29.419 --> 00:00:33.942
(e.g. USDC) can exist on several blockchains: Ethereum (the

00:00:33.942 --> 00:00:37.863
main one, higher fees) and cheaper Layer 2 networks

00:00:37.863 --> 00:00:40.877
such as Arbitrum, Base and Optimism.

00:00:40.877 --> 00:00:43.213
Next, the golden rule: the network you

00:00:43.213 --> 00:00:45.490
withdraw on must match a network your

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:48.366
wallet (or the receiving service) supports.

00:00:48.366 --> 00:00:51.306
Sending on the wrong network can mean lost funds.

00:00:51.306 --> 00:00:53.953
Then, Gas: every transaction pays a small

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:56.711
network fee in the network's gas token.

00:00:56.711 --> 00:01:00.061
On Ethereum and its Layer 2s that's ETH.

00:01:00.061 --> 00:01:02.075
Without a little ETH on the network

00:01:02.075 --> 00:01:04.501
you're using, you can't move anything.

00:01:04.501 --> 00:01:07.233
Also, your wallet address is usually the

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.566
same on Ethereum and its Layer 2s,

00:01:09.566 --> 00:01:12.731
but your balances are separate per network.

00:01:12.731 --> 00:01:15.166
Switch networks in your wallet to see them.

00:01:15.166 --> 00:01:17.512
On top of that, in your wallet, copy your

00:01:17.512 --> 00:01:20.026
address (use the copy button; never type it).

00:01:21.327 --> 00:01:24.539
And on the exchange, choose Withdraw → the coin

00:01:24.539 --> 00:01:27.885
→ pick the network (for learning, a low-fee Layer

00:01:27.885 --> 00:01:31.330
2 your wallet supports, e.g. Arbitrum or Base).

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.045
Plus, Paste the address.

00:01:33.045 --> 00:01:34.637
Check the first 6 and last

00:01:34.637 --> 00:01:36.765
6 characters match your wallet.

00:01:36.765 --> 00:01:38.360
Better, check it all.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.919
One more: Send a small test

00:01:40.919 --> 00:01:43.412
first (e.g. $10 of ETH).

00:01:43.412 --> 00:01:45.504
One more: Wait for it to arrive; see

00:01:45.504 --> 00:01:47.727
it in your wallet on that network.

00:01:47.727 --> 00:01:50.262
And finally, only then send the rest.

00:01:50.262 --> 00:01:52.357
Add the address to the exchange allowlist.

00:01:53.657 --> 00:01:56.527
Let's look at the diagram: Your first transfer.

00:01:56.527 --> 00:01:58.087
Take a moment with it.

00:01:58.087 --> 00:02:00.497
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:02:01.797 --> 00:02:04.312
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:02:04.312 --> 00:02:07.215
Withdrawing $100 of ETH to

00:02:07.215 --> 00:02:12.247
Arbitrum: exchange withdrawal fee ~$0.10–$1.

00:02:12.247 --> 00:02:14.345
Later, a swap on Arbitrum costs

00:02:14.345 --> 00:02:15.891
a few cents in gas.

00:02:15.891 --> 00:02:18.231
The same swap on Ethereum might cost a

00:02:18.231 --> 00:02:21.111
few dollars, more when the network is busy.

00:02:21.111 --> 00:02:23.246
That's why beginners learn on Layer 2s.

00:02:24.546 --> 00:02:26.006
Here's your checklist.

00:02:26.006 --> 00:02:27.541
Do it now, for real.

00:02:27.541 --> 00:02:29.701
First, Network chosen on purpose,

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:31.716
and supported by my wallet.

00:02:31.716 --> 00:02:35.776
Next, Address pasted, first and last 6 characters checked.

00:02:35.776 --> 00:02:39.176
Then, Test amount sent and received before the rest.

00:02:39.176 --> 00:02:40.860
Also, A little ETH held

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.844
on that network for gas.

00:02:42.844 --> 00:02:46.629
And finally, Address added to the exchange withdrawal allowlist.

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.140
Question 1.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.065
What's the golden rule of withdrawals?

00:02:55.485 --> 00:02:57.933
The answer: The network you send on must

00:02:57.933 --> 00:03:00.800
be one the receiving wallet or service supports.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.345
Question 2.

00:03:03.345 --> 00:03:07.750
You have USDC on Arbitrum but zero ETH there.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:08.680
Can you send it?

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:15.513
The answer: No. You need a little

00:03:15.513 --> 00:03:17.430
ETH on Arbitrum to pay gas.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:20.000
Question 3.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.365
Why send a test amount?

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:28.413
The answer: To confirm the address and network

00:03:28.413 --> 00:03:30.705
are right before risking the full amount.

00:03:32.005 --> 00:03:33.250
Let's recap.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:36.563
Networks (chains): the same token (e.g.

00:03:36.563 --> 00:03:40.094
USDC) can exist on several blockchains.

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:42.568
The golden rule: the network you withdraw

00:03:42.568 --> 00:03:44.688
on must match a network your wallet

00:03:44.688 --> 00:03:47.109
(or the receiving service) supports.

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:49.963
Gas: every transaction pays a small network

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:52.274
fee in the network's gas token.

00:03:52.274 --> 00:03:54.839
Your wallet address is usually the same

00:03:54.839 --> 00:03:56.699
on Ethereum and its Layer 2s.

00:03:57.999 --> 00:04:00.514
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:04:00.514 --> 00:04:03.126
Next up, Lesson 0.7: Your first

00:04:03.126 --> 00:04:05.819
DeFi steps (practice mode first).
